@h1 Architecture h1@

Wit is not a monolith. It is four — optionally five — pure-ish functions in a
line, each with a named, inspectable output type. Source text goes in one end;
a string comes out the other. Once you see the shape you know where to
intervene: swap the data loader, re-theme the renderer, write a renderer of
your own, or catch an error at the right boundary.

@h2 The pipeline h2@

@@@code parse code@@@ turns source text into a @code Document code@ AST.
@@@code loadExternalData code@@@ (optional) rewrites @@@code @load code@@@ nodes
into data. @code resolve code@ binds every use to the definition or data it
names and merges partials and cross-file references. @code expand code@
evaluates — it inlines definition bodies, substitutes interpolations and body
slots, runs conditionals and iteration, and runs @@@code <% %> code@@@ scripts.
@code renderHtml code@ and @code renderMarkdown code@ walk the
fully-substituted tree to a string.

@@@pre
 source ──parse──▶ Document ──loadExternalData──▶ Document ──resolve──▶ ResolvedDocument
   str            (@witlang/parser)   (optional, @witlang/runtime)      (@witlang/runtime)
                                                                              │
                                                                            expand
                                                                       (@witlang/runtime,
                                                                        runs <% %> scripts)
                                                                              ▼
   str ◀──renderHtml / renderMarkdown── ExpandedDocument
        (@witlang/render-html / -markdown)
pre@@@

Three things to read off the diagram. The dashed @@@code loadExternalData code@@@
step sits @em between em@ parse and resolve. Scripts run at the @strong tail of
expand strong@ — not as their own stage. Rendering is a pure tree walk: no I/O,
no configuration beyond @code mode code@ and @code css code@.

Each stage owns one artifact: characters become tokens, tokens become a raw AST,
the resolver binds every reference, the expander inlines definitions and
evaluates logic, and a renderer turns the expanded AST into an output string.

@h2 The stages h2@

@table |schema [Stage, Input, Output, Package, May throw]| |rows [[parse, string, Document, parser, WitError], [loadExternalData, Document + source, Document, runtime, E_LOAD_FAILED], [resolve, Document, ResolvedDocument, runtime, ResolverError], [expand, ResolvedDocument, ExpandedDocument, runtime, ExpanderError], [render, ExpandedDocument, string, render-html/-markdown, none]]|

Order is @strong parse → loadExternalData → resolve → expand → render strong@,
exactly as the CLI does it. @@@code loadExternalData code@@@ is optional: skip
it and a document with no @@@code @load code@@@ behaves identically, so call it
always or never — your choice.

@h2 Which package owns what h2@

@ul
@li @strong parser strong@ — lexer, parser, AST types, and errors. li@
@li @strong runtime strong@ — resolver, expander, data loader, and the script bridge. li@
@li @strong render-html strong@ and @strong render-markdown strong@ — the two reference renderers. li@
@li @strong cli strong@ — the @code wit code@ command that chains the stages. li@
@li @strong vscode strong@ — the extension and language server, running the same stages in the editor. li@
ul@

@h2 Where tools stop early h2@

Renderers consume @strong any strong@ post-expand AST — they never re-parse.
Tools that only need structure, such as the language server and a formatter, stop
after parse or resolve; they don't need expansion. This is why the boundary
between stages is a real interface, not an implementation detail.

@h2 The six-line pipeline h2@

The whole machine, wired by hand:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';                    // → Document
import { loadExternalData, resolve, expand } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { renderHtml } from '@witlang/render-html';          // → string

const doc      = parse(source, 'file.wit');       // parse(source, file?='<inline>')
const loaded   = loadExternalData(doc, dataSrc);  // optional: rewrites @load → DataDef
const resolved = resolve(loaded, { rootPath });   // → ResolvedDocument
const expanded = expand(resolved);                // → ExpandedDocument (runs scripts too)
const html     = renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document' });
pre@@@

For a document that needs no external data and no cross-file references, the
whole thing composes into one line:

@@@pre
renderHtml(expand(resolve(parse('Hello from a *def*.'))))
// → <article class="wit-doc"><p>Hello from a <strong>def</strong>.</p></article>
pre@@@

@h2 Watch the tree change shape h2@

Each stage's output is a plain object you can inspect (@code wit tour code@ prints
it as a readable tree). A pipeline inspector over one source makes the passes
concrete — log the block kinds, then the definitions and bindings the resolver
found, then the block kinds that survive expansion:

@@@pre
const src = '#greeting\nHello from a def.\ngreeting#\n\n@greeting';

const doc      = parse(src, 'demo.wit');
const resolved = resolve(doc);
const expanded = expand(resolved);

doc.children.map(c => c.kind);          // ['nodeDef', 'nodeUse']
[...resolved.definitions.keys()];       // ['greeting']
resolved.bindings.size;                 // 1
expanded.children.map(c => c.kind);     // ['paragraph']   ← the def is gone
pre@@@

The definition disappears because @code expand code@ inlined its body at the
use site. That is the whole story of the middle of the pipeline, in four lines.

@h2 Resolution timing h2@

Not everything resolves at the same moment — this three-tier model is worth
knowing before you build on the runtime.

@ul
@li @strong Eager. strong@ Pipe values, record right-hand sides, and conditional
operands outside definition bodies are computed once by the resolver and bound. li@
@li @strong Expansion-time. strong@ A definition body is re-resolved on every use,
so its interpolation and captured values reflect that call's arguments. li@
@li @strong Snapshot. strong@ An iteration freezes its collection at loop entry;
the body then reads from that frozen sequence. li@
ul@

@h2 Design notes worth knowing h2@

@ul
@li @strong Immutability. strong@ The resolver never mutates the parser AST.
Bindings live in a side table — @@@code ResolvedDocument.bindings: Map<NodeUse, Binding> code@@@
— not on the nodes. li@
@li @strong Re-resolution. strong@ @code expand code@ re-resolves uses by
@em name em@ rather than reading that side table, because inlined bodies produce
fresh @code NodeUse code@ clones the table never saw. li@
@li @strong Scripts are part of expand. strong@ The @code lh code@ bridge is
built and @@@code <% %> code@@@ scripts run at the tail of @code expand code@,
so renderers never encounter a script node. li@
@li @strong Inspectability. strong@ @code ResolvedDocument code@ carries
@code Map code@s (@code definitions code@, @code dataDefs code@,
@code bindings code@, @code partials code@), so log @code .keys() code@ or
@code .size code@ rather than @code JSON.stringify code@ to see inside them. li@
ul@

@h2 Structural guarantees h2@

Source locations — file, line, column, and length — ride every node through every
stage, so an error or a highlight always points at an exact place in the source.
Output is deterministic, and the AST is a serializable, exhaustive union, which is
what lets tools and renderers attach at any stage boundary.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Handing a renderer a @code ResolvedDocument code@. Definitions,
@@@code (each) code@@@, and @@@code (if) code@@@ are still present; renderers
expect an @code ExpandedDocument code@. TypeScript catches it — the types are
distinct on purpose. li@
@li Assuming a renderer re-parses. It consumes the expanded AST; it never touches
source. li@
@li Expecting @@@code loadExternalData code@@@ to be required. It is a no-op for
documents with no @@@code @load code@@@. li@
@li Expecting expansion at parse time. Parsing produces a raw tree with references
and logic intact; expansion is a separate, later stage. li@
@li Expecting scripts to run at render time. They ran during @code expand code@;
their effects are already baked into the tree. li@
ul@

